      "requirement": "Step 460: A study opens a tabulated material as it opens a crystal, and a component freed from a table that carries uncertainties takes its source \u03c3 as its prior (001 \u00a7D 'Uncertainties travel with the material (PTB's \u03c3) and become priors in studies and inputs to the Planner'; 001 \u00a7I 'a study OPENS a material version, which creates a study-local working copy ... with Unknown components'; 001 \u00a7N 'a material complexity or item model publishes its ParameterAxis list'; the S13.2 brief 'A study that opens such a material (S11.1) takes sigma as its components' prior'). Step 361 opened crystal-parameter versions only ('A complexity tree publishes no parameter axis in this spec'); a single tabulated isotropic segment now publishes its rows, and every other complexity is still refused. The copy keeps ONE list (`Study.materials`), so every reader of a study's opened materials and their `parameters` reads a table's components with no change: `libraryReferences` (step 359), `withMaterialParameterState` and `openedMaterialsIn` (step 361), `unknownsWith` (step 362), `parameterLayoutWith` (step 372), `SetOpenedMaterialParameter` (step 409), `EditStudyParameter` (step 411), the unknowns table (step 414), the fit run's parameters (step 383) and the promotion subjects (step 407); the S11.3 residual adds a row for each prior (step 387), so the source \u03c3 weighs a fit with no engine change. Each doc is one to three lines citing spec 0050 S13.2. (1) `OpticalConstructor.Domain/ItemModels.fs`: `ParameterName` (steps 205, 244) gains, beside step 244's position members, `static member tableRowIndex (row : int) : ParameterName` (`row<i>.n`) and `static member tableRowExtinction (row : int) : ParameterName` (`row<i>.k`), 1-based. (2) `OpticalConstructor.Domain/Studies.fs`, directly before `OpenedMaterial` (step 358): `OpenedMaterialPhysics` = `OpenedCrystalPhysics of CrystalParameters` | `OpenedTablePhysics of TabulatedNkTable`, the working copy's physics, stored in the serializer's case form as `CrystalParameters` is (step 359), with: `member this.componentNames : ParameterName list` (a crystal's `parameterAxes` names in order, step 361; a table's `tableRowIndex i` then `tableRowExtinction i` for each row in order); `member this.componentValue (name : ParameterName) : ParameterValue option` (the generated axis's `parameterValueOf`, or the row's n or k); `member this.withComponent (name : ParameterName) (value : ParameterValue) : OpenedMaterialPhysics` (written through the axis's `writeValue`, or the table with that row's n or k replaced in new columns; an unknown name answers `this`); `member this.componentSigmaOpt (name : ParameterName) : ParameterSigma option` (a table row's \u03c3n or \u03c3k when `uncertaintyOpt` holds a positive one, step 453; otherwise `None`); `member this.withoutSourceUncertainty : OpenedMaterialPhysics` (a table with `uncertaintyOpt = None`, a crystal unchanged); `member this.crystalOpt : CrystalParameters option`; and `member this.materialPhysics : MaterialPhysics` (`CrystalParameterPhysics c`, step 355, or `ComplexityPhysics` of one isotropic dispersive segment over `t.sampleRange` whose axis is `TabulatedNK t`, with no magnetic or active part, the shape every tabulated import builds, step 454). (3) `OpenedMaterial` holds `physics : OpenedMaterialPhysics` in place of `crystal : CrystalParameters`. `openFrom` (step 361): a `CrystalParameterPhysics crystal` opens as `OpenedCrystalPhysics crystal` with the parameters it has today; a `ComplexityPhysics` whose `eps` is `EpsWithDispValue (IsotropicDispersive [ s ])` with `s.dispersion = TabulatedNK t` and no magnetic or active part opens as `OpenedTablePhysics t` whose parameters are, per `componentNames` in order, `KnownParameter` of `componentValue`; any other complexity is `MaterialWithoutParametersErr` as today. `withParameterState` keeps its rules and, for a component whose `componentSigmaOpt` is `Some \u03c3`, stores an `UnknownParameter d` whose `d.priorOpt` is `None` as `UnknownParameter { d with priorOpt = Some { centre = <the opened physics' componentValue>; sigma = \u03c3 } }`: the opened version's value at its source's \u03c3; an explicit prior is kept and a known or calibrated state is unchanged. `currentCrystal` becomes `member this.currentPhysics : OpenedMaterialPhysics`, the physics with each component written through `withComponent` at its parameter's current number, in order. (4) `OpticalConstructor.Domain/StudyForward.fs`: `ExperimentAssignment` (step 373) holds `physics : Map<MaterialVersionId, OpenedMaterialPhysics>` in place of `crystals`; `crystalOf` becomes `member this.physicsOf (material : OpenedMaterial) : OpenedMaterialPhysics` (its entry or the copy's `currentPhysics`); `materialProxyOver` answers the store's entry with `physics = (physicsOf <the copy>).materialPhysics`, so a stack sample made of an opened table is solved with the study's working table at every wavelength; `assignmentFor` (b) writes each entry through `withComponent` and (c) reads a material leader through `componentValue`. `observedCrystalOpt` (step 375) is the one copy's `physicsOf \u2026 .crystalOpt`, so an observation of a table plate answers `ObservationPlateErr`, as an object of no opened crystal does. (5) `OpticalConstructor.Domain/StudyPublishing.fs`: `promotedMaterialWith` (step 407) answers `{ entry with physics = <the copy with its fitted components>.currentPhysics.withoutSourceUncertainty.materialPhysics }`: a fitted table's uncertainty is its fit run's (S11.4), not its source's. (6) Stored files. The study file keeps schema version 1: `study.schema.json` leaves the inner shapes to the serializer (step 359) and no study file has been released (.spec-md \u00a77). The committed `data/absorbing-biaxial-noisy/AbsorbingTriclinic.study-json` (step 438) is rewritten to the bytes the kit writes for its study, its one opened material's `crystal` key becoming `physics` holding `OpenedCrystalPhysics` of the same crystal and nothing else changing, so AT-2's file-by-file comparison (step 438) holds; `data/optics-mueller/QZ-LR.study-json` opens no material (steps 421-430) and keeps its bytes. (7) Tests. Every `OpenedMaterial` record expression writes `physics = OpenedCrystalPhysics <its crystal>` and every read of a copy's `crystal` reads its `physics`, every assertion and number unchanged: `KitStudyFileTests` and `KitContentFileJsonTests` (step 359), `StudyMaterialTests` (step 361, whose `the generated axes of an opened absorbing triclinic material equal AbsorbingTriclinicParameters.axes` reads `c.parameterAxes` of the copy's `OpenedCrystalPhysics c`), `StudyTests` (step 362), `StudyMessageTests` (steps 366, 409), `StudyForwardTests` (steps 373, 375: `physicsOf` in place of `crystalOf`), `StudyPublishingTests` (step 407), `StudyWindowModelTests` (step 410), `StudyViewTests` (step 414) and `KitAcceptanceTests`' `buildAbsorbingKit` (step 435: the generated axes of the copy's crystal). `a material whose physics is a complexity tree, or one opened twice, is refused` keeps its constant isotropic complexity refused. New facts over a test-local user material whose one isotropic tabulated segment holds 400, 500 and 700 nm, n 1.5, 1.6, 1.9 and k 0, 0.1, 0.3, with \u03c3n 0.01, 0.02, 0.03 and \u03c3k 0, 0.001, 0.002 (step 453). `StudyMaterialTests`: `a tabulated material opens with one known component per row's n and k`: it opens as `OpenedTablePhysics` of its table with `row1.n`, `row1.k`, `row2.n` \u2026 `row3.k` known at those values; a two-segment tabulated complexity answers `MaterialWithoutParametersErr`. `a freed component of an uncertain table takes its source sigma as its prior, and an explicit prior is kept`: freeing `row2.n` from 1.6 within [1.5, 1.7] with no prior stores the prior centre 1.6, \u03c3 0.02; freeing `row1.k` (\u03c3 0) stores none; a prior given explicitly is kept; and the same material without uncertainties frees `row2.n` with none. `an opened